ENFORCEMENT DECREE OF THE ACT ON THE PREVENTION OF STEEP SLOPE DISASTERS

CHAPTER I GENERAL PROVISIONS

Article 1 (Purpose)
The purpose of this Decree is to prescribe matters delegated by the Act on the Prevention of Steep Slope Disasters and matters necessary for its enforcement.

Article 2 (Definition of Steep Slopes)
Steep slopes under subparagraph 1 of Article 2 of the Act on the Prevention of Steep Slope Disasters (hereinafter referred to as the "Act") shall be as follows: <Amended on June 20, 2017; August 6, 2024>
1. Artificial slopes at least five meters high from ground level [meaning three meters where the shortest distance between the slope and a building, such as a house, is three meters or less], with a gradient of at least 34 degrees and a length of at least 20 meters;
2. Natural slopes at least 50 meters high from ground level, with a gradient of at least 34 degrees;
3. Other artificial slopes, natural slopes, or mountainous districts that a management agency, Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Mayor (including the mayor of an administrative city under Article 11 (1) of the Special Act on the Establishment of Jeju Special Self-Governing Province and the Development of Free International City; hereinafter the same shall apply), head of a Gun, or head of a Gu (meaning the head of an autonomous Gu; hereinafter the same shall apply) deems necessary to manage for disaster prevention.

Article 2-2 (Disclosure of Results of Safety Inspections of Steep Slopes)
A management agency shall, pursuant to Article 5 (2) of the Act, disclose on its website for at least 12 months the results of safety inspections, including the following matters:
1. Names and locations of the steep slopes on which safety inspections were conducted;
2. Period during which the safety inspections were conducted;
3. Details of the results of the safety inspections and plans for measures to be taken;
4. Other matters relating to the results of safety inspections that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary to disclose.
[This Article newly inserted 2021.4.6]

Article 2-3 (Scope and Methods of Surveys on the Actual State of Steep Slopes, etc.)
(1) A survey on the actual state of steep slopes under Article 5-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "survey") shall include the following matters:
1. Locations and sizes of steep slopes;
2. Types of slopes (whether they are natural or artificial slopes);
3. Factors posing a risk of collapse of steep slopes;
4. Other matters the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary for the survey.
(2) In principle, a survey shall be conducted directly on site, but indirect surveys using materials, literature, etc. and remote sensing using aircraft, satellites, etc. may be conducted in parallel.
(3) Details concerning surveys, other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) and (2), shall be determined by the Minister of the Interior and Safety.
[This Article newly inserted 2024.8.6]

Article 3 (Disaster Risk Assessments, etc.)
(1) A management agency [including a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, Mayor, head of a Gun, or head of a Gu (hereinafter referred to as the "head of a Si/Gun/Gu") that becomes a management agency pursuant to the latter part of Article 6 (2) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply] shall conduct disaster risk assessments under Article 6 (1) and (2) of the Act in consideration of the following matters: <Amended on June 20, 2017>
1. Risk of collapse, including the height, gradient, and history of collapse of the steep slope;
2. Social impacts, including the surrounding environment and distance from public facilities, etc.
(2) Where a management agency determines, as a result of a disaster risk assessment under paragraph (1), that designation as an area at risk of collapse is necessary, it shall give public notice of the following matters in the Official Gazette, the official bulletin for the area under its jurisdiction, etc. to collect residents' opinions and shall notify the owner, possessor, or manager of the relevant land (hereinafter referred to as a "person concerned"):
1. Scope of the area to be designated as an area at risk of collapse;
2. Restricted activities or prohibitions within the area at risk of collapse;
3. An improvement plan for the area at risk of collapse.
(3) Specific criteria for the matters to be considered in disaster risk assessments under the subparagraphs of paragraph (1) shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on November 19, 2014; July 26, 2017>

Article 4 (Continuous Measurement Management)
(1) A management agency or measurement service provider (meaning a person registered to engage in the measurement business pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Act; hereinafter the same shall apply) that performs continuous measurement management under Article 8 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as "continuous measurement management") shall install measuring instruments and manage data in accordance with the standard specifications for measurement of steep slopes prescribed by the Minister of the Interior and Safety in consultation with the heads of the relevant central administrative agencies. <Amended on November 19, 2014; July 26, 2017; August 6, 2024>
(2) A management agency or measurement service provider performing continuous measurement management, and the head of a Si/Gun/Gu provided with measurement data pursuant to Article 8 (2) of the Act, shall retain and manage the measurement data until the designation of the relevant area as an area at risk of collapse is revoked. <Amended on June 20, 2017; August 6, 2024>

Article 5 (Consultation on Activities)
The documents that a relevant administrative agency shall prepare when requesting consultation with the competent management agency under Article 10 (2) of the Act shall be as follows:
1. A project location map;
2. Project planning documents;
3. A document stating the purpose and necessity of the project;
4. A report analyzing the effects of the activity subject to consultation on the area at risk of collapse;
5. A document stating measures to ensure the stability of the area at risk of collapse.

Article 6 (Medium-Term Plans for Improvement of Areas at Risk of Collapse)
(1) A medium-term plan for improvement of areas at risk of collapse established by a management agency under Article 12 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "medium-term plan") shall include the following matters:
1. Basic information on steep slopes subject to improvement;
2. Information on occurrences of collapse and damage at steep slopes subject to improvement;
3. Measures for repair and reinforcement of steep slopes subject to improvement;
4. Estimated project costs necessary for improvement of steep slopes subject to improvement;
5. Improvement plans by steep slope subject to improvement and by year;
6. Other matters necessary for safety management before implementation of improvement projects.
(2) Where a management agency notifies the head of a Si/Gun/Gu of a medium-term plan under Article 12 (1) of the Act, it shall also submit a summary of the medium-term plan. <Amended on June 20, 2017>

Article 7 (Implementation Plans for Improvement Projects for Areas at Risk of Collapse)
(1) An implementation plan for an improvement project established by a management agency under Article 13 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as an "implementation plan") shall include the following matters:
1. An outline of the implementation plan for the improvement project, including the project name, location, scale, costs, and period;
2. Design documents (including cost estimates based on the design);
3. Results of a review of overlap and connections with other projects;
4. An analysis of the effects of the project;
5. An annual investment plan;
6. A maintenance plan for the period after completion of the improvement project.
(2) Where a management agency submits an implementation plan to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu under Article 13 (4) of the Act, it shall also submit a summary of the implementation plan. <Amended on June 20, 2017>

Article 8 (Orders to Take Safety Measures)
Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu orders a person concerned to take safety measures under Article 17 (1) of the Act, the head shall issue to that person a written order to take safety measures stating the following matters: <Amended on June 20, 2017>
1. The results of the safety inspection;
2. The grounds for ordering safety measures;
3. The matters for which safety measures shall be taken;
4. The methods of taking safety measures;
5. The deadline for taking safety measures.

Article 9 (Emergency Measures in Areas at Risk of Collapse)
(1) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu has a person at a disaster site or a person residing nearby take emergency measures under Article 19 (1) of the Act, the head shall issue a written order for emergency measures to the person subject to the order and shall issue a certificate confirming emergency measures to a person who has complied with the order: Provided, That where emergency measures must be taken urgently, the head may order emergency measures orally. <Amended on June 20, 2017>
(2) Where the head of a Si/Gun/Gu intends to temporarily use land, buildings, structures, or other property belonging to another person (hereinafter referred to as "land, etc.") or to alter or remove obstacles under Article 19 (1) of the Act, the head shall notify the person concerned in writing: Provided, That notification may be given orally in urgent cases, and where the person to be notified cannot be identified, the fact that land, etc. have been temporarily used or obstacles have been altered or removed shall be posted on the bulletin board of the relevant Si/Gun/Gu for at least 15 days.
(3) A public official who temporarily uses land, etc. or alters or removes obstacles under Article 19 (1) of the Act shall carry identification indicating the official's authority and present it to the person concerned.

Article 10 (Submission of Completion Documents and Current Status Data)
(1) Completion documents that a person who has created a steep slope shall submit to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu under Article 20 (1) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended on June 20, 2017; January 5, 2021>
1. General information on the steep slope, including its location, height, length, and gradient;
2. As-built drawings of the steep slope, including the geological characteristics and cracking condition of its exposed face, the status of application of surface repair and reinforcement methods, the installation of drainage channels, etc.;
3. Photographs of the steep slope upon completion.
(2) Current status data for steep slopes that a management agency shall submit to the competent head of a Si/Gun/Gu under Article 20 (2) of the Act shall include the following matters: <Amended on June 20, 2017>
1. General information, including the location, height, length, and gradient of the steep slope;
2. Ground information, including soil properties, rock properties, groundwater, and vegetation conditions of the steep slope;
3. The damage history and reinforcement facilities of the steep slope;
4. Photographs showing the current condition of the steep slope.
(3) A person who has created a steep slope under Article 20 (1) of the Act shall submit completion documents to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu or to the agency that granted the various authorizations, permits, etc. under relevant statutes and regulations (hereinafter referred to as the "authorization and permit authority") within 30 days from the date of completion of the relevant project. In such cases, the authorization and permit authority that has received the completion documents shall submit them to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu without delay. <Amended on June 20, 2017>

Article 11 (Submission of Materials Concerning Soil Investigations, etc.)
(1) Construction works for which a project implementer shall submit materials concerning soil investigations, etc. to the head of a Si/Gun/Gu under Article 20 (3) of the Act mean construction works that fall under any of the following subparagraphs and involve tests such as soil investigations: <Amended on June 20, 2017; September 8, 2020>
1. Civil engineering works with total construction costs of at least 500 million won (referring to civil engineering works under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry);
2. Building construction works for a building with a total floor area of at least 660 square meters (referring to building construction works under attached Table 1 of the Enforcement Decree of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry);
3. Specialized construction works with total construction costs of at least 200 million won (referring to specialized construction works under Article 2, subparagraph 6 of the Framework Act on the Construction Industry).
(2) The project implementer of construction works falling under paragraph (1) shall submit materials concerning soil investigations, etc. to the relevant administrative agency within 30 days from the date of completion, and the head of the relevant administrative agency that has received such materials shall submit them to the competent head of a Si/Gun/Gu without delay. <Amended on June 20, 2017>
(3) Matters necessary for the submission of materials concerning soil investigations, etc. under paragraph (2) shall be prescribed and publicly notified by the Minister of the Interior and Safety. <Amended on November 19, 2014; July 26, 2017>

Article 12 (Registration of Measurement Businesses)
(1) A person seeking registration to engage in continuous measurement management as a business pursuant to Article 22 (1) of the Act shall meet the requirements prescribed in Attached Table 1.
(2) A measurement service provider shall register changes where any of the following matters changes:
1. Its trade name;
2. The location of its principal place of business or a branch;
3. The representative or officers of the corporation;
4. Its personnel or equipment.
(3) The Special Metropolitan City Mayor, a Metropolitan City Mayor, a Special Self-Governing City Mayor, a Do Governor, or a Special Self-Governing Province Governor (hereinafter referred to as a "Mayor/Do Governor") shall grant registration and issue a certificate of registration of a measurement business as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, except where an application for registration under Article 22 (1) of the Act falls under any of the following cases: <Amended 2011.12.13, 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.6.20, 2017.7.26>
1. Where the requirements under paragraph (1) are not met;
2. Where the applicant for registration falls under any subparagraph of Article 23 of the Act;
3. Where any other restriction under the Act, this Decree, or other statutes and regulations is violated.
(4) Where a measurement service provider loses its certificate of registration of a measurement business or the certificate is damaged, it may have the certificate reissued as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>

Article 13 (Succession to the Status of a Measurement Service Provider)
(1) A measurement service provider that intends to acquire the business of another measurement service provider or merge with a corporation that is another measurement service provider pursuant to Article 24 (1) of the Act shall submit a report to the Mayor/Do Governor in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, accompanied by documents capable of proving that its personnel, equipment, etc. meet the criteria for registration of a measurement business, its certificate of registration of a measurement business, and the documents specified for each of the following cases: <Amended 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>
1. For a report of transfer and acquisition: A copy of the contract;
2. For a report of merger:
(a) A copy of the merger agreement;
(b) The public notice of the merger;
(c) A copy of the resolution of the general meeting, etc. approving matters concerning the merger.
(2) A person who intends to succeed to the status of a deceased measurement service provider and engage in a measurement business pursuant to Article 24 (3) of the Act shall submit a report to the Mayor/Do Governor in the form prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, accompanied by documents capable of proving that its personnel, equipment, etc. meet the criteria for registration of a measurement business, the certificate of registration of the measurement business, and documents capable of proving that the person is an heir. <Amended 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>

Article 14 (Registration of Performance Inspection Agents)
(1) A person seeking registration to perform inspections of the performance of measuring instruments on behalf of another pursuant to Article 27 (1) of the Act shall meet the registration criteria prescribed in Attached Table 2.
(2) A person registered to perform performance inspections on behalf of another pursuant to Article 27 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as a "performance inspection agent") shall register changes where any of the following matters changes:
1. Its trade name;
2. The location of its principal place of business or a branch;
3. The representative or officers of the corporation;
4. Its personnel or equipment.
(3) A Mayor/Do Governor shall grant registration and issue a certificate of registration as a performance inspection agent as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety, except where an application for registration under Article 27 (1) of the Act falls under any of the following cases: <Amended 2011.12.13, 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26, 2020.9.8>
1. Where the registration criteria under paragraph (1) are not met;
2. Where the applicant for registration falls under any subparagraph of Article 28 of the Act;
3. Where any other restriction under the Act, this Decree, or other statutes and regulations is violated.
(4) Where a performance inspection agent loses its certificate of registration as a performance inspection agent or the certificate is damaged, it may have the certificate reissued as prescribed by Ordinance of the Ministry of the Interior and Safety. <Amended 2013.3.23, 2014.11.19, 2017.7.26>

Article 15 (Public Notice of Registration of Measurement Businesses, etc.) A Mayor/Do Governor shall publicly announce the relevant fact in the official bulletin without delay in the following cases:
1. Registration of a measurement business or registration of changes thereto under Article 22 (1) of the Act;
2. A report of succession to the status of a measurement service provider under Article 24 (1) or (3) of the Act;
3. Revocation of registration of a measurement business or an order to suspend its business under Article 25 (1) of the Act;
4. Registration as a performance inspection agent or registration of changes thereto under Article 27 (1) of the Act;
5. A report of succession to the status of a performance inspection agent under Article 27 (3) of the Act;
6. Revocation of registration as a performance inspection agent or an order to suspend its business under Article 29 (1) of the Act.
[This Article wholly amended 2020.9.8]

Article 15-2 (Duties of the Korea Steep Slope Safety Association) The term "matters prescribed by Presidential Decree" in Article 32-2 (5) 8 of the Act means the following matters:
1. Conducting on-site investigations, including basic and detailed investigations, for the prevention of steep slope damage;
2. Conducting surveys, safety inspections, and disaster risk assessments to identify new steep slopes;
3. Providing on-site support in the event of a disaster, including prompt investigation of the causes of damage;
4. Research and development of measurement technologies for continuous measurement management of steep slopes;
5. Exchange and cooperation with domestic and foreign organizations related to steep slopes;
6. Policy research and development for the safety management of steep slopes.
[This Article newly inserted 2020.6.9]
[Former Article 15-2 moved to Article 15-7 <2020.6.9>]

Article 15-3 (Matters to Be Stated in the Articles of Incorporation of the Korea Steep Slope Safety Association) The matters to be stated in the articles of incorporation under Article 32-3 (1) of the Act of the Korea Steep Slope Safety Association under Article 32-2 (1) of the Act (hereinafter referred to as the "Association") shall be as follows:
1. Its purposes;
2. Its name;
3. The location of its office;
4. Matters concerning its business;
5. Matters concerning membership qualifications and membership fees;
6. Matters concerning assets and accounting;
7. Matters concerning officers and employees;
8. Matters concerning the general meeting and the board of directors;
9. Matters concerning its organizational structure;
10. Matters concerning amendments to its articles of incorporation;
11. Other matters necessary for the operation and management of the Association.
[This Article newly inserted 2020.6.9]
[Former Article 15-3 moved to Article 15-8 <2020.6.9>]

Article 15-4 (Number and Terms of Office of Officers of the Association, etc.)
(1) The officers of the Association shall consist of a president, vice presidents, directors, and auditors, with a total number not exceeding 20.
(2) The president and auditors shall be elected at the general meeting, and other officers shall be elected as prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
(3) The term of office of an officer shall be 3 years. Provided, That the term of an officer elected to fill a vacancy shall be the remainder of the predecessor's term.
(4) Matters necessary concerning the reappointment of officers, etc., other than those prescribed in paragraphs (1) through (3), shall be prescribed by the articles of incorporation.
[This Article newly inserted 2020.6.9]

Article 15-5 (Supervision of the Association) The Association shall report the following matters to the Minister of the Interior and Safety:
1. Important matters resolved by the general meeting and the board of directors;
2. Matters concerning the status of its members;
3. Other important matters concerning the operation of the Association and its members that the Minister of the Interior and Safety deems necessary.
[This Article newly inserted 2020.6.9]

Article 15-6 (Entrustment of Duties)
(1) The Minister of the Interior and Safety shall entrust the following duties to the Association pursuant to Article 33 (2) of the Act:
1. Conducting surveys under the former part of Article 5-2 (1) of the Act;
2. Preparation and dissemination of drawings and documents, etc. concerning standards for improvement projects in areas at risk of collapse under Article 13-2 of the Act.
(2) Mayors/Do Governors, heads of Sis/Guns/Gus, and management agencies (excluding local governments; hereinafter the same shall apply in this Article) may entrust the following duties to the Association pursuant to Article 33 (2) of the Act:
1. Conducting safety inspections of steep slopes under Article 5 (1) and (4) of the Act;
2. Conducting surveys under the former part of Article 5-2 (1) of the Act (limited to Mayors/Do Governors);
3. Conducting disaster risk assessments under Article 6 (1) and (2) of the Act;
4. Establishing databases under Article 20 (3) of the Act (limited to heads of Sis/Guns/Gus).
(3) Where a Mayor/Do Governor or the head of a Si/Gun/Gu entrusts duties to the Association under paragraph (2), the Mayor/Do Governor or the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall publicly notify the details of the entrusted duties.
(4) Where a management agency entrusts duties to the Association under paragraph (2), it shall submit the details of the entrusted duties to the head of the Si/Gun/Gu, and the head of the Si/Gun/Gu shall publicly notify those details.
[This Article newly inserted 2024.8.6]
[Former Article 15-6 moved to Article 15-7 <2024.8.6>]

Article 15-7 (Procedures and Methods for Selection of Agents for Disaster Prevention Management Measures, etc.) Where a management agency intends to select an agent for disaster prevention management measures under subparagraph 14 of Article 2 of the Countermeasures against Natural Disasters Act pursuant to Article 33-2 (1) of the Act, it shall select the agent through an evaluation based on the criteria for assessment of project performance capabilities in Attached Table 2-2.
[This Article newly inserted 2020.6.9]
[Moved from Article 15-6; former Article 15-7 moved to Article 15-8 <2024.8.6>]

Article 15-8 (Processing of Unique Identifying Information) The Minister of the Interior and Safety (including the heads of specialized institutions or organizations designated and publicly notified pursuant to Article 30 (2) of the Act) and Mayors/Do Governors (including persons to whom the relevant authority has been delegated or entrusted, where such authority is delegated or entrusted) may, where unavoidable for the performance of the following duties, process data containing resident registration numbers under subparagraph 1 of Article 19 of the Enforcement Decree of the Personal Information Protection Act: <Amended 2014.11.19, 2017.6.20, 2017.7.26>
1. Duties concerning verification of grounds for disqualification of measurement service providers under Article 23 of the Act;
2. Duties concerning verification of grounds for disqualification of performance inspection agents under Article 28 of the Act;
3. Duties concerning preliminary practical training for specialized measurement personnel under Article 30 of the Act.
[This Article newly inserted 2014.8.6]
[Moved from Article 15-7; former Article 15-8 moved to Article 15-9 <2024.8.6>]

Article 15-9 Deleted <2026.3.24>

Article 16 (Imposition of Administrative Fines) The criteria for imposition of administrative fines under Article 37 (1) of the Act shall be as prescribed in Attached Table 3.

